      "choice_text": "The strands store different sequence information because their base orders differ; A–T and C–G pairing determines each complementary sequence.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "DNA stores information in the order of its nucleotide bases, not simply in which bases are present. Specific complementary pairing allows each strand’s base sequence to determine the sequence of a partner strand, supporting information copying."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "The strands store the same sequence information because they contain equal numbers of each base; A–T and C–G pairing determines each complementary sequence.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Base-count trap: Equal base counts do not mean equal sequence information because the bases occur in different orders. Fix: Compare the bases position by position rather than comparing only their totals."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "The strands store different sequence information in the order of their sugars and phosphates; A–T and C–G pairing determines each complementary sequence.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Backbone-as-code trap: DNA has a repeating sugar–phosphate backbone; the variable base sequence stores the information. Fix: Identify which nucleotide component varies along each strand, then compare the order of that component."
     },
